[11:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'give people access to as many of the tools that they need from those 3D engines to tell a story natively on the web. Um, but to also keep it as accessible as possible where they can do so without writing any code. They don\'t'

[11:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need experience in these engines. Uh, a lot of the actual um inspiration for for doing this all came from students in journalism. So like my background for spatial storytelling was doing photoggramometry'

[11:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as a a research assistant my senior year at Syracuse University and we told like a really compelling story that won a bunch of awards. So like that\'s the core of what I\'m aiming for and it\'s since you know bifurcated to some some'

[12:06] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Gabriele Romagnoli speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'different um verticals I\'d say and you know you have the real estate side and you have the more like um yeah cultural preservation side but then journalism um is is where like the passion sort of is'

[12:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and journalists don\'t really know how to use 3D editing tools usually um and you need to be able to do this relatively quickly. So, the idea is like you take a scan of something on scene and then you'

[12:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can go tell that story with found audio and other info and additional photos and all the things you need to and turn that around really quickly. Um, early on in my career, I worked for Yahoo Sports and'

[12:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I did a lot of augmented reality embeds for their like spatial storytelling on their web, like on their web pages. So, we would be uh tied in with like a story about Steph Curry breaking the three time uh all-time three-point shot record'

[12:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and we\'d have a a 3D shot chart. Uh or we do all those types of things. Um and when I was there, I was really the only one that was native 3D or uh you know, software engineer. Everybody else was a'

[13:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'journalist being trained to do these things. And that\'s sort of part of the inspiration of where this came from. But the idea is to keep it, you know, at its core something that everybody can use while still being powerful enough to to do the things that are needed to be'

[13:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'done. &gt;&gt; And I think you you brought up an angle that is fascinating because I haven\'t thought about it. So, of course, real estate, it\'s obvious, right? And uh and what is also obvious in in some way is like the business behind it, right? I I think it\'s straightforward. For'

[13:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heritage, it\'s a bit less in a way, right? I think that there is this need about preserving this thing and capturing and telling stories. business is a little bit more blurry. Yeah. &gt;&gt; But when it comes to journalism, I think'

