[14:15] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Gabriele Romagnoli speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'that needs to go into adding this extra 3D layer to their story. So I think there\'s, you know, probably two ways that you could go about this as a journalist. And there are sort of'

[14:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'opposite ends of the spectrum. I I think a, you know, the majority of small newsrooms, um, people doing just, uh, you know, smaller stories that need to have quick turnaround times can just do an inbed. And I think that works really'

[14:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well, especially if it complements the article that you\'re you\'re writing. Um, and for that, like, you know, with Story Splat or Super Splatter or other tools, you can just copy the embed code and put it in your website. You can also host them yourself. So, a lot of news rooms'

[14:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to be able to host the entire thing and not have anything on my servers. That works. Um, and you can do complete offline hosting. Uh, but then on the flip side of that, I think there\'s a lot of more rich storytelling that can be done if you have the time and the budget'

[15:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to do so. So, you know, uh, the New York Times does amazing content. The New Yorker, I\'ve worked with, um, uh, Cleared by Fire u was a piece that was put out by the New Yorker last fall. Um,'

[15:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
